# unified-brain — settings.
# Copy this file to .env in the same folder as codebase_qa_service.py and
# next_claude.py.
#
# Part 1 is the configuration the evals in evals/ ran on; fill in
# the keys and you have our setup. Part 2 lists every other constant the
# code reads, with its default; leave them out unless you want to change one.
#
# Comments go on their own lines, or after a value and a space. Never after
# an empty value: python-dotenv reads "KEY=   # text" as the value "# text".

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PART 1 — the eval configuration (For a quick start simply fill the keys in PART 1)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The repository's database file. `python next_claude.py /path/to/repo`
# writes /path/to/repo/.unified-brain.db; point the service at that file.
DB_PATH=/path/to/repo/.unified-brain.db

# Writer / judge model: writes the answers to questions and the verdict of
# every probe in a report. llmbase = DeepSeek V4.1 Flash on Fireworks; or anthropic.
LLM_PROVIDER=llmbase
LLM_API_KEY=
# Writer list price, $ per million tokens, so the footer can print the
# writer's cost next to the judge's. Not sent anywhere; only multiplied.
# Fireworks DeepSeek V4.1 Flash: 0.22 / 0.66. Isoquant 1017-fast: 0.035 / 0.30.
# DeepInfra Qwen3.5-27B: 0.26 / 2.60. Leave empty to omit the two fields.
WRITER_PRICE_IN=0.22
WRITER_PRICE_OUT=0.66

# The decision model on OpenRouter: scores skills, follows names through
# the code, picks the same-commit changes the judge needs. Any OpenRouter
# model that answers the Decisions API can be named; the one alternative we
# measured (cloudflare/clef-flash) kept 2 of 16 seeds on our test question
# and its chains wandered — not recommended.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Default: pplx-decider (Perplexity, Apache-2.0, 27B, $0.02 / M) — the
# CHEAP profile below. typesafe/jev-1.14 is the COVERAGE judge: +1.25
# points on the xz evals at about 3x the judge cost
# (evals/results/qa-xz-2026-11-06-pplx.md vs qa-xz-2026-11-05-b.md).
JEV_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2
#JEV_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.14

# Code vectors: qwen (Qwen3-Embedding-8B on DeepInfra; the evals) or
# voyage (Voyage code 4, about 20x the price; same answers on our tests).
# The ingest's embedding pass fills CODE_EMBED_PASS2; questions pre-filter
# with QA_VECTOR_STORE. Keep both the same.
CODE_EMBED_PASS2=qwen
QA_VECTOR_STORE=qwen
DEEPINFRA_API_KEY=
VOYAGE_API_KEY=

# Chains. JEV_HIT_TRIAGE=1: when a followed name is found in several places,
# Jev first scans them in one call (coordinates + the lines around each
# occurrence, judged against each other) and only the places it would open
# are read in full — 3–8x fewer Jev calls on a large repository, same
# answers; 0 reads every place. JEV_NAMES_AS_CHOICE=1: the names of a body
# are offered to Jev as ONE choice question (a distribution over the names
# plus "none of these") instead of one yes/no question per name — about 5%
# fewer Jev tokens, same answers; a name is followed when it beats "none of
# these" and has at least half the best name's share. 0 restores one
# question per name.
# =======> Both set in the Profiles block (Part 2).

# With JEV_HIT_TRIAGE=1: a followed name found in fewer places than this is
# read in full; from this many places on, the triage picks. 2 triages every
# group; 26 (JEV_EXPAND_MAX_HITS + 1) reads small sets whole and triages only
# the common names. Note JEV_HIT_TRIAGE=0 is not "read everything": a name
# found in more than JEV_EXPAND_MAX_HITS places then links nothing.
JEV_TRIAGE_FROM=2



# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PART 2 — tuning constants (defaults shown run automatically through 
# the code, no need to uncomment; uncomment ONLY to experiment)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Profiles ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Four measured settings for questions. Use one block whole; the knobs are
# tuned together. Every number below is copied from the source named in
# its block (evals/results/); nothing is estimated. Profile D's source is
# not an eval file yet, see its block. xz, nine questions, 74 points. "judge" = the decision model's cost from the answer footer,
# "writer" = the writer call at the list price of the provider used in
# that run. Time is wall-clock per answer, question submitted to answer
# complete.
#
#   profile                 score        cost, nine questions     time per answer             points lost: writer / evidence gathering
#   A  FULL                 70    / 74   see block A (footers     36–130 s                    4.0  = 2.5  / 1.5
#                                        of three questions
#                                        were not recorded)
#   B  CHEAP                68.75 / 74   $0.253                   519 s / 9 = 58 s (41–119)   5.25 = 1.75 / 3.5
#   C  HYBRID-RELIABLE      66.5  / 74   $0.135                   44 s (21–109)               7.5  = 2.5  / 5.0
#   D  ULTRA-FAST LOCAL     62.0  / 74   $0.161                   10.1 s (6.2–16.4)           12.0 = not tallied (see below)
#
#   For comparison, frontier coding agents on the same nine questions
#   (one fresh agent per question, shell access to a checkout of xz):
#   Fable 5.1, high effort  69    / 74   $17.55 (measured)        193 s (1,736 s / 9)         —
#   Fable 5.2, max effort   72.75 / 74   about $50 (estimated,    597 s (5,372 s / 9)         —
#                                        not a bill; a floor)
#
# "Writer" = the fact was in the evidence and the answer did not use it.
# "Evidence gathering" = the fact did not reach the writer (judge said no,
# the chain did not get there, or the search did not return it).
# Sources of the split: A — qa-xz-2026-11-05-b-pplx.md ("Run f ... lost 4.0:
# writer 2.5, judge 1.0, reach 0.5"); B — qa-xz-2026-11-06-pplx.md, "Where
# the points were lost" (writer 1.75; judge 1.5, chain 1.0, knn 0.5, index
# 0.25, cap 0.25); C — qa-xz-2026-11-12-reliable-hybrid.md (writer 2.5;
# judge 4.0, reach 1.0). D: the split of the Jev-judge run was not tallied;
# the same profile with the pplx judges
# (qa-xz-2026-11-13-ultrafast-local-k40-pplx.md) lost 11.75 = writer 4.05 /
# evidence 7.7 (judge 3.85, reach 3.85).
# Agents: Fable 5.1 — qa-xz-2026-10-29.md; Fable 5.2 —
# qa-xz-2026-11-13-fable-5-2-agents.md. An agent gathers and writes in one
# loop, so its losses have no such split.
#
#
# A and B were run with the writer on Fireworks ($0.22 / $0.66 per M) and
# xz at 45d08abc; C and D with the writer on Isoquant ($0.035 / $0.30 per M)
# and xz at 7baf6835. Costs are therefore not comparable across that line
# without re-pricing the writer.
#
# Shared by all four profiles:
JEV_HIT_TRIAGE=1
JEV_NAMES_AS_CHOICE=1
JEV_EXPAND_MAX_ADD=200
JEV_TRIAGE_FROM=2
JEV_QUESTION_GREP=0
# JEV_BATCH_SIBLINGS=1: the candidates under one parent are judged in one
# call — question, chain and ancestors' code sent once instead of once per
# sibling. Same questions per candidate. Measured on xz Q4 (date, result).
#JEV_BATCH_SIBLINGS=1
#
# ── A  FULL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Score : 70 / 74 (94.6 %), 0 wrong claims.
# Cost  : judge $0.605 on the seven questions whose footers were recorded
#         (Q1, Q2, Q3, Q6, Q7, Q8, Q9; $0.053–$0.109 each). Judge + writer
#         $0.067–$0.144 per answer on the six questions with complete
#         footers (Q1, Q2, Q3, Q7, Q8, Q9). No nine-question total exists.
# Time  : 36–130 s per answer on those six; evidence gathering 15–20 s.
# Calls : about 3,900 judge calls per nine questions (400 seed scores each
#         plus chains).
# Source: evals/results/qa-xz-2026-11-05-b.md (run f). Writer on Fireworks,
#         WRITER_REASONING at its default. xz at 45d08abc.
# To use: comment the four CHEAP lines and the JEV_MODEL line in Part 1,
# uncomment these:
#JEV_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.14
#JEV_PREFILTER_TOP_K=400
#JEV_PREV_BODY_MAX_CHARS=60000
#JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=0
#JEV_RETRY_BAND=0.10
#
# ── B  CHEAP (active) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Score : 68.75 / 74 (92.9 %), 1 wrong claim.
# Cost  : $0.253 for the nine = judge $0.092 + writer $0.161;
#         $0.010–$0.048 per answer.
# Time  : 519 s for the nine, 41–119 s per answer; evidence gathering
#         206 s for the nine, 12–36 s per answer.
# Calls : 949 judge calls for the nine.
# Source: evals/results/qa-xz-2026-11-06-pplx.md. Writer on Fireworks,
#         WRITER_REASONING at its default. xz at 45d08abc.
# With JEV_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2 (Part 1):
JEV_PREFILTER_TOP_K=20
JEV_PREV_BODY_MAX_CHARS=15000
JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=1
JEV_RETRY_BAND=0
#
# ── C  HYBRID-RELIABLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The fast judge follows the chains; the full judge takes the three
# decisions that are final: which seeds to keep, which places to open, and
# whether a held lead reaches the writer (JEV_FINAL_MODEL; without it a
# large build file such as CMakeLists.txt is reached and then dropped at
# the last step — xz Q1, 4 runs of 4 fixed).
# Score : 66.5 / 74 (89.9 %), 4 wrong claims.
# Cost  : $0.135 for the nine = judge $0.095 + writer $0.040;
#         $0.015 per answer on average ($0.0065–$0.0198).
# Time  : 396 s for the nine, 44 s per answer on average (21–109 s);
#         evidence gathering 12 s on average (8–18 s).
# Source: evals/results/qa-xz-2026-11-12-reliable-hybrid.md. Writer on
#         Isoquant, WRITER_REASONING at its default. xz at 7baf6835.
# To use: comment the four CHEAP lines and the JEV_MODEL line in Part 1,
# uncomment these:
#JEV_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-flash-v1.2
#JEV_SELECT_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2
#JEV_TRIAGE_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2
#JEV_FINAL_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2
#JEV_PREFILTER_TOP_K=100
#JEV_MIN_SCORE=1.9
#JEV_PREV_BODY_MAX_CHARS=15000
#JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=1
#JEV_RETRY_BAND=0
#
# ── D  ULTRA-FAST LOCAL (Jev judge) ────────────────────────────────────
# The question's vector is computed on this machine by Ollama; pplx-decider
# scores the 40 nearest candidates; Jev follows the chains for two hops and
# does the triage; the writer answers without reasoning first.
# Score : 62.0 / 74 (83.8 %), 4 wrong claims (all on Q8).
# Cost  : $0.161 for the nine = judge $0.134 + writer $0.027;
#         $0.018 per answer on average.
# Time  : 90.7 s for the nine, 10.1 s per answer on average (6.2–16.4 s).
# Source: second full run of 2026-11-13, all nine questions in their
#         original wording. NOT YET IN evals/results/ — the figures are
#         from that day's grading notes; write the eval file before
#         quoting them. A first run the same day scored 63.5 / 74 with Q7
#         asked with an added clause (84.7 s, $0.164 for the nine).
#         Writer on Isoquant. xz at 7baf6835. Question vector:
#         Qwen3-Embedding-8B, 4-bit, by Ollama on a Mac M3, 16 GB.
#         The lines below are rebuilt from that session, not copied from
#         the .env of the run; check them against it.
# The same profile with the pplx judges instead of Jev IS in evals/results/
# (qa-xz-2026-11-13-ultrafast-local-k40-pplx.md): 62.25 / 74 with the same
# Q7 clause, $0.065 for the nine (judge $0.043 + writer $0.022), 12.1 s per
# answer on average. For it set JEV_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-flash-v1.2
# and JEV_TRIAGE_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2 below.
# To use: comment the four CHEAP lines and the JEV_MODEL line in Part 1,
# uncomment these (Ollama must be running with the model pulled):
#JEV_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.14
#JEV_TRIAGE_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.14
#JEV_SELECT_MODEL=perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.2
#JEV_PREFILTER_TOP_K=40
#JEV_MIN_SCORE=1.9
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_HOPS=2
#JEV_FINAL_PASS=0
#JEV_SEARCH_AHEAD=1
#JEV_CONCURRENCY=256
#JEV_PREV_BODY_MAX_CHARS=15000
#JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=1
#JEV_TRIAGE_NAMES=1
#JEV_TRIAGE_REOPEN=1
#JEV_RETRY_BAND=0
#QA_PLAIN_MARKERS=1
#WRITER_REASONING=none
#QUERY_EMBED_OLLAMA=qwen3-embedding:8b
#QUESTION_VECTOR_CACHE=0

# ── Ingest / embedding pass ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Embedding batches sent at the same time.
#CODE_EMBED_PASS2_WORKERS=4

# ── Questions: embedding pre-filter ────────────────────────────────────
# Nearest skills by code vector that Jev then scores. If the vector search
# fails, Jev scores every skill instead. Set in the Profiles block above
# (20 cheap / 400 coverage). At 20 the chains rebuild the set on broad
# questions; narrow questions with 2–4 seeds have no slack (xz Q5 lost a
# point). The log line "kept seeds at knn ranks" shows yours.
# Cosine score a vector row needs to count (per store).
#MIN_SCORE_VOYAGE=0.35
#MIN_SCORE_QWEN=0.35
# Most vector rows read while collecting the nearest skills.
#CODE_KNN_MAX_ROWS=20000
# QUERY_EMBED_OLLAMA=<model name>: the question's vector is computed on this
# machine by Ollama (at OLLAMA_URL, default http://localhost:11434) instead
# of the hosted model. Qwen store only; stored vectors and ingestion are not
# touched. If Ollama fails or returns another vector size, the hosted model
# is used. Empty = off. Profile D uses it with JEV_PREFILTER_TOP_K=40.
#QUERY_EMBED_OLLAMA=
#OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434
# QUESTION_VECTOR_CACHE=1: the vector of a question is stored the first time
# it is asked (per embedding model) and read back after that, so a repeated
# question makes no embedding call.
#QUESTION_VECTOR_CACHE=0

# ── Questions: Jev scoring of the candidates ───────────────────────────
# Score (0–4) a skill needs to be kept.
#JEV_MIN_SCORE=2.0
# Optional second judge for the two places where few calls decide a lot:
# the seed scoring (JEV_SELECT_MODEL) and the hit triage (JEV_TRIAGE_MODEL).
# Empty = same model as JEV_MODEL.
#JEV_SELECT_MODEL=
#JEV_TRIAGE_MODEL=
# JEV_SEARCH_AHEAD=1: the first hop's name searches run for every candidate
# while the judge is still scoring them. Speed only. Default 0.
#JEV_SEARCH_AHEAD=0
# Most skills kept, and most characters of their bodies, for one question.
#JEV_MAX_SKILLS=60
#JEV_MAX_CHARS=300000
# Jev calls sent at the same time when scoring.
#JEV_CONCURRENCY=128
# Greps run at the same time inside a chain hop.
#JEV_GREP_CONCURRENCY=16
# 1: the judge picks the names to follow from each seed body; 0: open every 1–4-definition name blindly
#JEV_SEED_NAMES=1        

# ── Chains: Jev follows names out of the code (questions and probes) ───
# 0 switches the chains off for questions.
#JEV_EXPAND=1
# How many hops a chain may take from its start.
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_HOPS=5
# JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=1: the definitions a body uses go through triage (signature
# and first lines of each, one call per body) instead of being read whole in
# text order and cut at JEV_EXPAND_PER_NODE; the judge says per definition
# whether to open it, and reads go highest probability first. Default 0.
#JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=0
# JEV_TRIAGE_NAMES=1: in that triage each definition is shown with the names
# its whole body uses, not only its first lines. Only acts with
# JEV_TRIAGE_DEFS=1. Default 0.
#JEV_TRIAGE_NAMES=0
# JEV_TRIAGE_REOPEN=1: a place the triage skipped under one name stays open
# to a later link. Without it the place counts as already read, and a name
# the judge then chooses to follow finds nothing (xz Q7: `io_open_src_real`).
# A bug fix; default 0 keeps older evals comparable.
#JEV_TRIAGE_REOPEN=0
# Lines of a place the triage sees: this many each side of the line that
# names the followed name, or the first 2x this many lines of a definition.
#JEV_TRIAGE_AROUND=3
# JEV_FINAL_PASS=1: every open step that was followed is judged once more at
# the end, now also seeing what it led to. 0 (speed): no second judgment;
# open steps keep their first verdict. JEV_FINAL_MODEL names the model for
# that judgment; empty = JEV_MODEL.
#JEV_FINAL_PASS=1
#JEV_FINAL_MODEL=
# Probability Jev must give a place (hit triage) or a name (when
# JEV_NAMES_AS_CHOICE=0) for it to be followed. 0.7 saved 8% of the chain
# cost on our broadest test question and lost the first and last stage of
# the answer; leave it at 0.5.
#JEV_FOLLOW_MIN=0.5
# A body Jev calls a derived copy (translation catalog, mirrored or
# generated file) is a dead end when its "derived" probability is above
# this. Run d (2026-11-01): the highest value on a real body was 0.51,
# on cmake/tuklib_physmem.cmake — a false yes; 0.65 keeps that out.
#JEV_DERIVED_MIN=0.65
# A "reject" below this probability is treated as "continue" (not sure).
#JEV_SURE_REJECT=0.75
# Jev's probabilities move 1–7 points between identical calls (xz Q9,
# 2026-11-02: two runs on the same code, same bodies). A judgement that
# lands on the strict side of a cut but within this band — a place dropped
# by the hit triage, a "reject" just over JEV_SURE_REJECT, a "derived" just
# over JEV_DERIVED_MIN — is asked once more and the more permissive answer
# is kept. Every flip seen so far dropped a true positive; none let a
# wrong body in. Fires on a handful of bodies per question. 0 disables.
#JEV_RETRY_BAND=0.10
# Score (0–4) a reached body needs to be attached as evidence.
#JEV_EXPAND_MIN_SCORE=2.0
# Per question: most Jev calls, most bodies attached, most characters attached.
#JEV_EXPAND_BUDGET=300
# Was 60. xz Q4, run b (2026-11-01): the expansion reached and accepted
# the two bodies the answer needed, but the footer showed "+60 bodies" —
# the cap — and both were left out (4.5 / 7). At 200: +63 bodies, 7 / 7,
# $0.003 more. Characters are still bounded by JEV_EXPAND_MAX_CHARS.
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_ADD=200
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_CHARS=250000
# A name with more grep hits than this is "everywhere" and is not followed.
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_HITS=25
# Most definitions of one name that are read.
#JEV_EXPAND_MAX_DEFS=4
# Most names taken from one body, and lines around the link they are taken from.
#JEV_EXPAND_PER_NODE=30
#JEV_EXPAND_WINDOW=10
# Most names offered to Jev from one body.
#JEV_FOLLOW_POOL=40
# Text one Jev call may hold. A longer body shows the part around the link.
#JEV_BODY_MAX_CHARS=60000
# Characters of each *ancestor* body re-shown in a chain judgement; the
# body being judged is shown whole. Set in the Profiles block above.
# Possible cut seen: xz Q9, hardware.c's last function (−0.25).
# Reached-but-not-read places listed as leads.
#JEV_LEADS_MAX=20
# Who judges each reached body: jev, or llm (the writer model; slower, costs more).
#CHAIN_JUDGE=jev
# Judge calls sent at the same time when CHAIN_JUDGE=llm.
#JUDGE_CONCURRENCY=16

# ── Probes (reports) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Chains started from the changed lines: 3 = three chains, one search and
# one Jev call per chain and hop. 0 = the wide search (80 Jev calls per hop).
#PROBE_FAST_CHAINS=3
# Per probe: most Jev calls, most bodies attached by the chains.
#PROBE_FAST_CHAIN_BUDGET=80
#PROBE_FAST_CHAIN_ADD=15
# Same-commit changes are shown in full up to this many characters; above
# it, Jev reads them whole and picks the ones the judge needs.
#PROBE_FAST_COMMIT_CHARS=150000
# Changes per Jev pick call.
#PROBE_FAST_PICK_BATCH=40
# Evidence budget per probe; blocks that do not fit are dropped and named.
#PROBE_FAST_EVIDENCE_CHARS=300000
# Changed lines of the target put in the chain question.
#PROBE_FAST_QUESTION_DIFF_CHARS=6000
# Diff lines shown to the judge; a longer diff shows head and tail, marked.
#PROBE_DIFF_MAX_LINES=6000
# Reasoning effort of the judge call (provider dependent).
#PROBE_FAST_EFFORT=high
# A skill touched by more commits than this in the range gets one probe per commit.
#PROBE_SPLIT_THRESHOLD=1
# 1 = other members of the target's composites join the first hop (composites
# are not formed by the local ingest, so this does nothing here).
#PROBE_FAST_COMPOSITES=0
# Probes run at the same time during a report (the code allows at most 20).
#REPORT_CONCURRENCY=4
# Time limit of one probe re-run from the timeline page, in seconds.
#PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1500
# Characters of each verdict's tail read by the cross-probe reconcile step.
#RECONCILE_TAIL_CHARS=1200
# A report's lease: another process may take it over this many seconds
# after the owner stops renewing.
#REPORT_LEASE_SECONDS=120

# ── Answers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Output limit of the writer call (thinking tokens count against it).
#WRITER_MAX_TOKENS=120000
# WRITER_REASONING=none: the writer answers without thinking first (speed).
# Default high. Only profile D sets it; its effect on the score has not
# been measured on its own.
#WRITER_REASONING=high
# QA_PLAIN_MARKERS=1: a file-level body is stored without the lines of its
# functions, with a marker in their place. For the writer the marker is
# rewritten to say what those lines are and where their body is in the
# evidence (or that it is not included). Default 0.
#QA_PLAIN_MARKERS=0
# QA_DUMP_CONTEXT=<file path>: the evidence text the writer receives is also
# saved to that file, to inspect one run. Empty = off.
#QA_DUMP_CONTEXT=

# ── Writer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each LLM_PROVIDER has a built-in model and endpoint: llmbase =
# openai/accounts/fireworks/models/deepseek-v4p1-flash at
# https://api.fireworks.ai/inference/v1; anthropic = anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5.
# Override either here — e.g. another Fireworks model, or any other
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint with LLM_PROVIDER=llmbase.
#LLM_MODEL=
#LLM_API_BASE=

# ── Service ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The service listens on http://127.0.0.1:PORT. The MCP config's
# UNIFIED_BRAIN_URL must match.
#PORT=8000